@@ -0,0 +1,60 @@
+# Windows worker lifecycle repair
+
+Base: Paperclip 0.3.1, source revision 5e55510dbeb869bacfcd843f975632dc739519af.
+
+The Windows adapter previously signalled its immediate child only. Detached nested
+children could survive parent exit, timeout and cancellation, including holding
+the parent's output pipes open. A disposable Node 22.23.2 fixture reproduced all
+three cases. No real provider, desktop action or Paperclip database was involved.
+
+## Execution boundary
+
+The existing runChildProcess Windows branch now launches a small private Python
+bridge using the installed interpreter selected by an absolute, operator-owned
+PAPERCLIP_WINDOWS_PYTHON setting. Missing configuration fails closed. No download,
+PATH search, policy override or service lifecycle is part of this change.
+
+Each attempt has an independent UUID and unnamed kernel Job Object with
+KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE and no breakaway permission. STARTUPINFOEX JOB_LIST assigns
+membership atomically at process creation; HANDLE_LIST limits inherited handles
+to worker stdio. The root remains suspended until onSpawn metadata is handled.
+The worker never receives the helper's control or protocol handles. Native argv,
+environment sanitization and adapter permission choices remain in the existing
+adapter path. Explicit native bypass=false remains effective.
+
+Cancellation, control EOF, deadline and natural root exit all terminate remaining
+job members and query ActiveProcesses until zero, with a five-second cleanup
+budget. The Node caller only confirms cleanup after the matching final receipt
+and successful helper exit. Missing receipts, malformed identity, query failure
+or helper crash report uncertainty. Closing the helper's sole job handle is the
+crash backstop. This is process lifetime containment, not a filesystem/network or
+desktop permission sandbox.
+
+Heartbeat cancellation uses the live attempt owner's stop operation on Windows.
+Persisted numeric PIDs do not authorize termination after owner loss. Agent-pause
+cancellation now waits for cleanup before updating cancelled status. There is no
+database schema change. Unix group signalling remains on its existing path.
+Retries of a run share the original deadline; cancelled/expired attempts cannot
+be relaunched under that run ID. Deadline zero retains existing unlimited-run
+semantics, while root-exit cleanup is always bounded.
+
+## Adoption owned by Hermes
+
+1. Obtain the independent review verdict for the exact fix commit and evidence.
+2. Cherry-pick the reviewed fix into the existing Paperclip source while preserving
+   Claude's onboarding changes and Hermes's MCP commit. Stop on overlapping edits.
+3. Set PAPERCLIP_WINDOWS_PYTHON in the existing service's owned launch environment
+   to the already-installed Python interpreter. Do not change worker permissions,
+   credentials, models, database, company or agents. No unrestricted mode.
+4. Build @paperclipai/adapter-utils with the existing Node/package environment;
+   retain src/windows-worker.py alongside dist (included in package files).
+   Typecheck/build the service using its existing workspace links before adoption.
+5. Hermes alone schedules any shared-service restart and real adapter acceptance.
+   Builder tests are inert worker fixtures; no provider/native desktop task was
+   dispatched and no installed service source/bundle was modified.
+
+Windows 10+ is required for PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_JOB_LIST. Unsupported native
+containment, interpreter failures and nested-job restrictions fail closed.
+
+References: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/job-objects
+and https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/processthreadsapi/nf-processthreadsapi-updateprocthreadattribute.
